    keyAdvance: The world's first liquid-propellant rocket, launched by Robert Goddard
      on March 26, 1926.
    triviaFact: Its historic first flight lasted only 2.5 seconds and the rocket
      landed in a nearby cabbage patch.
    spriteName: goddard_nell
  - id: v2_aggregat4
    displayName: V-2 (Aggregat-4)
    correctZoneId: 1942_1944
    zoneLabel: 1942 - 1944
    country: Germany
    keyAdvance: The first long-range ballistic missile and the first rocket capable
      of reaching space.
    triviaFact: The V-2 was the first man-made object to reach the edge of space.
    spriteName: v2_aggregat4
  - id: r7_sputnik
    displayName: R-7 / Sputnik Launcher
    correctZoneId: 1957
    zoneLabel: 1957
    country: Soviet Union (USSR)
    keyAdvance: The first orbital launch vehicle in history, launched on October
      4, 1957.
    triviaFact: The R-7 is the only rocket family that has been continuously flying,
      in evolved form, since the dawn of the Space Age - the longest-serving rocket
      design in history.
    spriteName: r7_sputnik
  - id: saturn_v
    displayName: Saturn V
    correctZoneId: 1967_1969
    zoneLabel: 1967 - 1969
    country: United States
    keyAdvance: The most powerful rocket ever flown successfully, with the capability
      to send astronauts to the Moon.
    triviaFact: At 363 feet tall, Saturn V was taller than the Statue of Liberty
      and generated the thrust of 85 Hoover Dams.
    spriteName: saturn_v
  - id: space_shuttle
    displayName: Space Shuttle (STS)
    correctZoneId: 1981
    zoneLabel: 1981
    country: United States
    keyAdvance: The first reusable orbital spacecraft, first launched on April 12,
      1981.
    triviaFact: Its Solid Rocket Boosters were recovered from the ocean after launch
      and flown again on later missions.
    spriteName: space_shuttle
  - id: falcon_9
    displayName: Falcon 9
    correctZoneId: 2015_2017
    zoneLabel: 2015 - 2017
    country: United States
    keyAdvance: The first orbital-class rocket booster to land and be reused.
    triviaFact: Falcon 9 achieved the first successful vertical landing of an orbital
      booster in December 2015, and the first re-flight of a used booster in March
      2017.
    spriteName: falcon_9
  - id: starship
    displayName: Starship
    correctZoneId: 2023
    zoneLabel: 2023
    country: United States
    keyAdvance: A fully reusable super-heavy lift vehicle designed for Moon and Mars
      missions.
    triviaFact: Starship is the largest rocket ever built, and its April 2023 flight
      was the first integrated test of the full vehicle.
    spriteName: starship
  rocketSprites:
  - {fileID: 21300000, guid: b4937e41fc8f643489bf864538f9f857, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 7ddfa3a82685cc44797018729149538c, type: 3}
